# a struct with a private owning property, constructed at file scope, inside a function and
# as a temporary. every one of those was refused with `'$cells' is private to 'Grid'` against a line the
# author did not write, and the assertions are the other half - the property has to be destroyed exactly
# once, not zero times and not twice
--- OUT --->
2
4
0
balanced
--- RAST --->
# each of the three positions owes one call and nothing more: no member access to `$cells` anywhere a
# `Grid` is torn down, which is what made every one of them a `PrivateMember` error
CHECK: call $deinit(addrof<Grid&>(varref<Grid>(var($g))))
CHECK: unwind call $deinit(addrof<Grid&>(varref<Grid>(var($g))))
CHECK: call $deinit(addrof<Grid&>(varref<Grid>(var($__temp1))))

# and the access is inside `Grid`'s own body, where its owner answers AST::enclosing_type_of and the
# ordinary `private` rule lets it through. one body, three call sites
CHECK: function $deinit -> type<void>
CHECK: call $deinit(addrof<array<int32>&>(ma<array<int32>>(varref<Grid&>(var($this))->cells)))
